You just focus on the shooter.

01:03:07.170 --> 01:03:08.670
Make sure he's good to go on the 6th.

01:03:08.930 --> 01:03:09.550
You'll be ready.

01:03:10.590 --> 01:03:12.610
Please welcome to the show Anthony Starr, everybody!

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:13.830
Thank you!

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:35.350
-Thanks for having me. I'm so pleased to be here.

01:03:35.350 --> 01:03:36.730
-This is your first one of these.

01:03:36.730 --> 01:03:39.430
-It's my first one of these, and I've been a fan of yours...

01:03:39.430 --> 01:03:41.430
-That's too kind. -...for quite a while,

01:03:41.430 --> 01:03:44.230
since the Foreign Correspondents Center in 2012.

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:47.130
-20 -- Yeah, that was -- Let's not talk about it.

01:03:47.130 --> 01:03:49.130
-We don't have to talk about that. -We don't talk about it.

01:03:49.130 --> 01:03:50.850
-I'm very -- I'm a fan of yours,

01:03:50.850 --> 01:03:53.050
and I have been since I first laid eyes on the show.

01:03:53.050 --> 01:03:54.350
This is season four. -Yeah.

01:03:54.350 --> 01:03:56.250
-From season one, Homelander,

01:03:56.250 --> 01:03:58.960
really one of the worst people on TV.

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:02.230
And so, so great to meet you backstage,

01:04:02.230 --> 01:04:04.690
and you're such an affable fella.

01:04:04.690 --> 01:04:06.430
And I realized, "Wow, you're a really good actor,"

01:04:06.430 --> 01:04:08.350
'cause he's a real piece of .

01:04:08.350 --> 01:04:13.890
-I think that's a kind way of putting it.

01:04:13.890 --> 01:04:15.590
Yeah, no, it's amazing, you know.

01:04:15.590 --> 01:04:17.010
I'm still getting people coming up going,

01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:18.510
"Oh, what? He's actually nice."

01:04:18.510 --> 01:04:20.320
-Yeah. -And, yeah, I don't fly.

01:04:20.320 --> 01:04:21.720
I don't laser people.

01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:23.520
I don't do any of those things.

01:04:23.520 --> 01:04:24.460
-This is -- It should be noted.

01:04:24.460 --> 01:04:26.460
For anyone who hasn't seen the show, you know,

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:28.220
we obviously are living in an era

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:30.000
of a lot of superheroes and pop culture.

01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:31.860
"The Boys" is unlike anything else.

01:04:31.860 --> 01:04:34.900
It really stands alone in its take on superheroes.

01:04:34.900 --> 01:04:38.300
Were you familiar with any of it before it came to you,

01:04:38.300 --> 01:04:39.700
like the comic it was based on or anything?

01:04:39.700 --> 01:04:41.940
-I wasn't familiar with the comic.

01:04:41.940 --> 01:04:43.540
I was actually filming something else

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:45.140
when they asked me to audition, and I was like,

01:04:45.140 --> 01:04:47.360
"I'm far too busy doing this other thing."

01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:49.660
And then -- That's true.

01:04:49.660 --> 01:04:52.610
And then -- But I finally put a tape down,

01:04:52.610 --> 01:04:55.810
almost out of spite, because my reps kept hassling me about it.

01:04:55.810 --> 01:05:00.000
I went to my trailer, and at lunchtime, I got my iPad out,

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:04.260
just literally spat an audition out, sent it away,

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:06.080
and went, "Right. Done. Back to work."

01:05:06.080 --> 01:05:08.480
And then, of course, they love it.

01:05:08.480 --> 01:05:11.860
And then I thought, "I better look at what it is."

01:05:11.860 --> 01:05:15.810
So I looked at the comic book and, "Oh, my -- my nerves."

01:05:15.810 --> 01:05:16.960
-Yeah. -I was like,

01:05:16.960 --> 01:05:18.740
"How the hell would I put this on TV?

01:05:18.740 --> 01:05:20.840
You can't scream that. This is obscene.

01:05:20.840 --> 01:05:24.460
Oh, my God." -It's -- It really is obscene.

01:05:24.460 --> 01:05:26.120
-It's obscene. -Yeah.

01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:28.030
-So the show's taken a lot of creative license.

01:05:28.030 --> 01:05:29.570
I think one of the beautiful things about the show

01:05:29.570 --> 01:05:30.000
is...

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:35.000
when it comes -- I've heard this phrase thrown around a lot.

01:05:35.000 --> 01:05:36.400
"Superhero fatigue."

01:05:36.400 --> 01:05:38.200
And I think we are the antidote to that

01:05:38.200 --> 01:05:40.700
because our universe is completely 180.

01:05:40.700 --> 01:05:42.100
-Yeah. -The good guys are the bad guys,

01:05:42.100 --> 01:05:43.400
and the bad guys are the good guys, right?

01:05:43.400 --> 01:05:46.570
So, I am -- Homelander is --

01:05:46.570 --> 01:05:48.490
Sorry, all the Homelander fanboys

01:05:48.490 --> 01:05:51.190
that thought he was the good guy.

01:05:51.190 --> 01:05:53.390
The giveaway was when he killed a guy on a plane.

01:05:53.390 --> 01:05:55.370
-Yeah, pretty early on. Pretty early on, yeah.

01:05:55.370 --> 01:05:58.180
-Episode one, season one. -Yeah.

01:05:58.180 --> 01:06:00.000
-But then, you know,

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:01.560
I don't think there is superhero fatigue.

01:06:01.560 --> 01:06:03.240
I think everyone's going to be really interested

01:06:03.240 --> 01:06:05.240
in storytelling when it's done right.

01:06:05.240 --> 01:06:06.800
And we've just got such a unique angle.

01:06:06.800 --> 01:06:09.200
-It's also nice, I think, for anybody who decides,

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:10.440
"Oh, I'm going to start from the beginning."

01:06:10.440 --> 01:06:12.770
Like, it's not based on anything.

01:06:12.770 --> 01:06:14.270
You know, if you start episode one, you're like,

01:06:14.270 --> 01:06:15.270
"You're in on a world."

01:06:15.270 --> 01:06:16.990
You didn't have to see another movie or another show.

01:06:16.990 --> 01:06:18.790
You're just like, "This is it." And that's really cool.

01:06:18.790 --> 01:06:20.590
-It's really cool, and it's really fun to be a part

01:06:20.590 --> 01:06:21.790
of something like that, you know?

01:06:21.790 --> 01:06:26.200
I mean, this character as well, we've developed it

01:06:26.200 --> 01:06:28.390
in such a way that it could literally go anywhere.

01:06:28.390 --> 01:06:30.000
-Yeah. -So I could cry like a little

01:06:30.030 --> 01:06:32.930
or I could be, like, a horrible beast for a day

01:06:32.930 --> 01:06:34.570
and then you just drop it all.

01:06:34.570 --> 01:06:36.870
-We've seen -- -I leave it in the spandex.

01:06:36.870 --> 01:06:37.770
-I'm gonna say something with --

01:06:37.770 --> 01:06:39.670
Oh, I hope I'm not giving anything away.

01:06:39.670 --> 01:06:41.870
There's been some breastfeeding scenes with you.

01:06:41.870 --> 01:06:49.800
And let me say, there's the weird part --

01:06:49.800 --> 01:06:51.700
-What's so cool about that? -Not flashbacks.

01:06:53.880 --> 01:06:57.810
-But I -- -But those scenes are so difficult.

01:06:57.810 --> 01:07:00.000
That's, like, the weirdest scene I've ever done.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:02.340
some pretty weird in my career. -Yeah.

01:07:02.340 --> 01:07:05.740
-But when we -- I read that, and I was like --

01:07:05.740 --> 01:07:09.340
I'm so used to reading, like, the bizarre things on this show.

01:07:09.340 --> 01:07:13.190
I read that, and I'm like, "Just another day at work."

01:07:13.190 --> 01:07:15.330
But then we get there, and I'm getting almond juice

01:07:15.330 --> 01:07:17.160
squirted in my face.

01:07:17.160 --> 01:07:20.900
And I just had this instinct that it would be --

01:07:20.900 --> 01:07:23.100
that it should -- I don't know. I don't know.

01:07:23.100 --> 01:07:25.000
A lot of the time, I don't know what I'm gonna do at work.

01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:27.040
-Yeah. -Like, I've prepped so much

01:07:27.040 --> 01:07:28.870
that we can just go anywhere.

01:07:28.870 --> 01:07:30.000
And it just started --

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:34.070
And it turned into a love scene, where I'm basically going,

01:07:34.070 --> 01:07:36.070
"Oh, my God, you did this for me."

01:07:36.070 --> 01:07:38.070
And she's all like, "I'd do anything for you."

01:07:38.070 --> 01:07:40.070
And it's like something out of the bloody notebook.

01:07:40.070 --> 01:07:42.070
-Yeah. -And it's --

01:07:42.070 --> 01:07:44.930
But it's about breast milk getting squirt in the face.

01:07:44.930 --> 01:07:46.930
And I remember talking to Valerie Currie,

01:07:46.930 --> 01:07:48.930
that plays "Firecracker." She's brilliant.

01:07:48.930 --> 01:07:50.750
And we were on the same page with her.

01:07:50.750 --> 01:07:54.750
I remember, after a take, just going, "What are we doing?"

01:07:54.750 --> 01:07:57.670
"I could have been a lawyer. I could have --"

01:07:57.670 --> 01:08:00.000
What is this job?

01:08:00.090 --> 01:08:02.350
-Uh, speaking of what are we doing,

01:08:02.350 --> 01:08:04.450
so I've had some of your cast members --

01:08:04.450 --> 01:08:06.250
Jack Quaid, Jensen Ackles, they've been on the show.

01:08:06.250 --> 01:08:09.450
And they've talked about Eric Kripke, the creator.

01:08:09.450 --> 01:08:12.190
If he finds out there's something that makes you uncomfortable

01:08:12.190 --> 01:08:15.490
as a performer, he will not do the polite thing

01:08:15.490 --> 01:08:18.310
of writing away from it. He will write towards it.

01:08:18.310 --> 01:08:20.310
-Mm. -You don't care for heights.

01:08:20.310 --> 01:08:22.420
-I'm not particularly fond of them.

01:08:22.420 --> 01:08:26.250
-All right, so here is a clip that you brought.

01:08:26.250 --> 01:08:27.690
This is -- -Oh, I forgot.

01:08:27.690 --> 01:08:28.950
-Yeah. -You've got the clip.

01:08:28.950 --> 01:08:29.800
So...

01:08:30.350 --> 01:08:33.590
Yeah, we'll beep it for people at home,

01:08:33.590 --> 01:08:40.950
but we're gonna let everybody in the audience listen to --

01:08:40.950 --> 01:08:42.650
All right, so here you are, Anthony,

01:08:42.650 --> 01:08:45.090
getting lifted up into the sky.

01:08:45.090 --> 01:08:47.480
- - off.

01:08:47.480 --> 01:08:49.390
Oh, off.

01:08:49.390 --> 01:08:53.270
Oh, my life.

01:08:53.270 --> 01:08:57.320
off.

01:08:57.320 --> 01:08:58.610
-No, not this.

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:02.580
He normally just lands.

01:09:02.580 --> 01:09:05.280
-Okay. Please, let's go.

01:09:05.280 --> 01:09:13.960
I was raised by pirates. What can I say?

01:09:13.960 --> 01:09:15.520
-It's a great cast, as I mentioned.

01:09:15.520 --> 01:09:17.460
But you were starstruck by one person,

01:09:17.460 --> 01:09:18.710
and I don't blame you.

01:09:18.710 --> 01:09:20.650
-Ooh.

01:09:20.650 --> 01:09:22.860
Yeah, so, when I was little --

01:09:22.860 --> 01:09:25.370
and she was actually not much older than me,

01:09:25.370 --> 01:09:28.270
but slightly, I was a big fan of "The Karate Kid."

01:09:28.270 --> 01:09:30.000
-Yeah. -I used to do karate

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:31.830
when I was a little -- a little kid.

01:09:31.830 --> 01:09:34.790
And my dad took me to see "The Karate Kid" at the movies.

01:09:34.790 --> 01:09:36.700
-Yeah. -And Ally,

01:09:36.700 --> 01:09:39.970
AKA Elizabeth Shue, was, like,

01:09:39.970 --> 01:09:42.290
probably my first on-screen crush.

01:09:42.290 --> 01:09:45.140
-This is such a weird crowd tonight.

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:48.280
-Yeah. You guys are so full of empathy.

01:09:48.280 --> 01:09:51.570
-Seconds ago, he was screaming you on camera.

01:09:51.570 --> 01:09:54.740
And now, all of a sudden, you're like, "Oh, oh."

01:09:54.740 --> 01:10:00.000
-I'll take it. Um, uh, no, so --

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:03.240
-So then, all of a sudden, she gets cast as Homelander's boss.

01:10:03.240 --> 01:10:05.890
-Yeah. -In this evil corporation.

01:10:05.890 --> 01:10:07.990
And I'm like, "I've got to work with Elizabeth Shue.

01:10:07.990 --> 01:10:09.330
Oh, my God. What am I going to do?

01:10:09.330 --> 01:10:11.230
What am I going to wear? Am I going to look okay?"

01:10:11.230 --> 01:10:16.660
And turns out she was -- she is the nicest person.

01:10:16.660 --> 01:10:21.260
Like, to the point that we were on set, and she was --

01:10:21.260 --> 01:10:23.900
you know, she was -- there was props, lots of props.

01:10:23.900 --> 01:10:25.760
They gacked her up with so much props.

01:10:25.760 --> 01:10:27.640
She was like, "I can't do this. I don't know how to do it."

01:10:27.640 --> 01:10:30.000
I was like -- she came up and she goes, "I'm feeling really

01:10:30.670 --> 01:10:31.910
"I'm not feeling good about this scene.

01:10:31.910 --> 01:10:32.910
I'm feeling really insecure."

01:10:32.910 --> 01:10:37.010
And I said, "You're Elizabeth Shue.

01:10:37.010 --> 01:10:39.650
Come to this little jerk and say that.

01:10:39.650 --> 01:10:42.690
I don't know. I'm trying to learn from you."

01:10:42.690 --> 01:10:45.890
-Well, I will tell you that it was fantastic.

01:10:45.890 --> 01:10:48.210
The two were fantastic. She was equally villainous.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:50.910
And it was wonderful to watch you guys together.

01:10:50.910 --> 01:10:52.010
And it's just such a great show.

01:10:52.010 --> 01:10:53.510
And I'm so happy you were here. Thanks so much.

01:10:53.510 --> 01:10:54.910
-Thanks, Ken. Appreciate it.

01:10:54.910 --> 01:10:56.660
-Pleasure.

01:10:56.660 --> 01:10:59.000
-New episodes of "The Boys are Available."

01:11:01.280 --> 01:11:05.520
Stick around. We'll be right back with Julia Phillips.

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:16.530
She's a critically acclaimed and best-selling author.

01:11:16.530 --> 01:11:19.530
Her new novel, "Bear," was published on June 25th.

01:11:19.530 --> 01:11:21.700
Please welcome to the show, Julia Phillips.

01:11:41.000 --> 01:11:43.000
-I am very excited to have you here.

01:11:43.000 --> 01:11:44.600
I was very excited to meet you backstage.

01:11:44.600 --> 01:11:45.900
I've loved two of your books.

01:11:45.900 --> 01:11:49.140
"Disappearing Earth" was a book that I recommended to everyone.

01:11:49.140 --> 01:11:50.920
And it was a National Book Award finalist,

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:52.460
so congratulations on that.

01:11:52.460 --> 01:11:54.150
-Thank you.

01:11:54.150 --> 01:11:56.920
-It was one of the books I was recommending

01:11:56.920 --> 01:11:58.630
during the pandemic, because I feel like people

01:11:58.630 --> 01:12:00.000
were really doing a lot of reading.

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:03.300
And this book, "Bear," was written during the pandemic.

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:04.200
-Yes, it was.

01:12:04.200 --> 01:12:06.640
-And I feel like this is not a book about the pandemic,

01:12:06.640 --> 01:12:09.340
but it is about characters who were affected by the pandemic.

01:12:09.340 --> 01:12:11.140
And I think a lot of people have recommended,

01:12:11.140 --> 01:12:12.840
"Oh, nobody wants to relive those days

01:12:12.840 --> 01:12:14.230
or think about that anymore."

01:12:14.230 --> 01:12:16.450
Was it -- Why did you think that was

01:12:16.450 --> 01:12:18.550
an important element to their story?

01:12:18.550 --> 01:12:20.250
-I have to say, I think that because I think

01:12:20.250 --> 01:12:21.650
it's an important element to my story.

01:12:21.650 --> 01:12:23.860
I don't know what everyone's 2020 experience was,

01:12:23.860 --> 01:12:25.560
but I would say mine was impactful.

01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:26.360
-Yeah.

01:12:26.360 --> 01:12:30.000
And I had my first kid in June, 2020.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:34.590
which I would not recommend having a baby in an active pandemic.

01:12:35.290 --> 01:12:38.990
And the combination of quarantine and postpartum,

01:12:39.050 --> 01:12:41.650
and I pretty much spent a year inside terrified.

01:12:42.350 --> 01:12:44.490
And it was not an experience that, I mean,

01:12:44.530 --> 01:12:46.150
as we all wanted to imagine,

01:12:46.290 --> 01:12:48.690
sort of went away after a couple weeks.

01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:51.890
It was an experience that I think if my grandkids come over

01:12:51.890 --> 01:12:53.710
and I do something, my kids are going to say,

01:12:53.770 --> 01:12:55.720
well, you know, Grammy's that way

01:12:55.720 --> 01:12:57.460
because she lived through the pandemic

01:12:57.460 --> 01:12:59.660
and that's why she is the way she is.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:02.240
It was very shaping. It was very foundational.

01:13:02.240 --> 01:13:03.840
And when I think of these characters,

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:06.340
this contemporary novel about these two sisters

01:13:06.340 --> 01:13:08.000
who live in this beautiful island,

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:09.840
but they're struggling making ends meet.

01:13:09.840 --> 01:13:11.740
Their mom has a lung disease.

01:13:11.740 --> 01:13:13.940
They have been very, very impacted

01:13:13.940 --> 01:13:15.340
by the fallout of the pandemic

01:13:15.340 --> 01:13:19.410
in ways that have shaped them, I think, forever.

01:13:19.410 --> 01:13:21.770
And so, it doesn't seem possible to me

01:13:21.770 --> 01:13:24.940
to write contemporary fiction without, at least for me,

01:13:24.940 --> 01:13:28.100
without having that be part of what has made us who we are today.

01:13:28.100 --> 01:13:30.000
-It is this story about sisters.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:31.760
And it's interesting because, again,

01:13:31.760 --> 01:13:32.960
we all lived through the pandemic,

01:13:32.960 --> 01:13:34.740
but we all had different experiences with it.

01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:36.940
And this is about two very close sisters

01:13:36.940 --> 01:13:39.650
who also have different relationships

01:13:39.650 --> 01:13:40.890
to their shared memories.

01:13:40.890 --> 01:13:44.200
Was that something you wanted to sort of explore,

01:13:44.200 --> 01:13:46.020
the idea that people who would assume

01:13:46.020 --> 01:13:47.520
the person they are closest with

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:49.520
has seen things exactly the same way as them?

01:13:49.520 --> 01:13:51.220
-Yeah, absolutely. I mean, I --

01:13:51.220 --> 01:13:53.530
This is a very foundational thing for me as a --

01:13:53.530 --> 01:13:54.770
I'm a little sister myself.

01:13:54.770 --> 01:13:56.330
I know you're an elder sibling, right?

01:13:56.330 --> 01:13:57.830
-Yeah. -It's a totally different dynamic.

01:13:57.830 --> 01:14:00.000
-Yeah, ours is harder.

01:14:00.570 --> 01:14:02.230
-You get to do whatever you want,

01:14:02.230 --> 01:14:04.710
and everybody is so excited to see you,

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:06.470
and they're like, "Oh, what have you been up to?"

01:14:06.470 --> 01:14:07.550
-And this seems right.

01:14:07.550 --> 01:14:10.150
And this seems right for an elder sibling to say something,

01:14:10.150 --> 01:14:11.690
and then you want the little one to say,

01:14:11.690 --> 01:14:13.210
"You're right, and I agree."

01:14:13.210 --> 01:14:17.650
And that's good. And that's good, and that's true.

01:14:17.650 --> 01:14:19.150
Yeah, there's this dynamic, this dynamic

01:14:19.150 --> 01:14:21.050
where you grow up in the same house with someone,

01:14:21.050 --> 01:14:23.150
or I think even for folks who don't have a sibling,

01:14:23.150 --> 01:14:25.940
like, any long-term relationship, any long-term friendship,

01:14:25.940 --> 01:14:27.260
you share your life with someone.

01:14:27.260 --> 01:14:28.500
You think, "I'm so close to you.

01:14:28.500 --> 01:14:30.000
We're closer than anyone could possibly

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:33.400
I know you so well, and yet we are not on the same page,

01:14:33.400 --> 01:14:36.100
and we perceive everything so differently,

01:14:36.100 --> 01:14:37.740
and your reality is not my reality.

01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:41.000
And for me, that was such an important part of being a sibling,

01:14:41.000 --> 01:14:42.460
such an important part of growing up,

01:14:42.460 --> 01:14:46.710
that when my elder sibling started to kind of grow away from me

01:14:46.710 --> 01:14:50.240
and get out of the house and begin his life first,

01:14:50.240 --> 01:14:55.120
that was a tough and intense moment to navigate.

01:14:55.120 --> 01:14:57.690
And they're navigating that in this story a little bit later

01:14:57.690 --> 01:14:59.530
because they've been living together for so long.

01:14:59.530 --> 01:15:00.000
They're not just

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:01.700
but best friends and roommates,

01:15:01.700 --> 01:15:03.340
and they're bonded by the shared experience

01:15:03.340 --> 01:15:05.200
of caring for their mom.

01:15:05.200 --> 01:15:07.890
And that has made them think for years,

01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:10.050
like, "Well, we understand each other. We get it."

01:15:10.050 --> 01:15:12.850
But there's so much that they don't understand.

01:15:12.850 --> 01:15:15.640
-This book takes place, you know,

01:15:15.640 --> 01:15:17.660
on an island in the Pacific Northwest.

01:15:17.660 --> 01:15:19.160
Disparing Earth took place

01:15:19.160 --> 01:15:21.490
in a very remote peninsula in Russia.

01:15:21.490 --> 01:15:26.220
Both books, for me as a reader, they sort of start slow.

01:15:26.220 --> 01:15:28.230
They give you a sense of place.

01:15:28.230 --> 01:15:30.000
You feel like it has this -- it's this way

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.240
one kind of book, and then they have

01:15:31.240 --> 01:15:32.900
this propulsive energy to them.

01:15:32.900 --> 01:15:36.230
They become page-turners in a way you didn't expect.

01:15:36.230 --> 01:15:38.030
And then, I mean, I will say, like,

01:15:38.030 --> 01:15:40.920
I had to fight the urge to flip ahead.

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:43.120
It was, like, the wrong kind of page-turner for me,

01:15:43.120 --> 01:15:45.620
where I'm like, "Oh, it's gonna be a whole, like, chapter flipper."

01:15:45.620 --> 01:15:48.140
Do you write, wanting people to just be, like,

01:15:48.140 --> 01:15:49.940
dying to know what's gonna happen?

01:15:49.940 --> 01:15:52.850
-Very much. I mean, that's the experience I want as a reader.

01:15:52.850 --> 01:15:54.250
That's the experience I'm always chasing.

01:15:54.250 --> 01:15:57.850
Of course, I want to write something with beautiful sentences

01:15:57.850 --> 01:16:00.000
that is communicated to the

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:03.440
reader and deep emotional relationships that moves a reader.

01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:07.440
But I want all of that to be delivered in the sort of vehicle

01:16:07.440 --> 01:16:09.680
that is a story where you can't turn any pages.

01:16:09.680 --> 01:16:12.240
To me, that is the most thrilling reading experience,

01:16:12.240 --> 01:16:14.960
and it's the one I'm always chasing as a writer.

01:16:14.960 --> 01:16:16.800
-I'm not giving anything away, because the title does.

01:16:16.800 --> 01:16:18.180
There's a bear in this book.

01:16:18.180 --> 01:16:19.580
-There's a bear in this book.

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:20.880
-And I like -- because this is --

01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:22.340
this is not false advertising.

01:16:22.340 --> 01:16:23.840
Because there's a TV show called "The Bear,"

01:16:23.840 --> 01:16:26.380
and it's just a chef.

01:16:26.380 --> 01:16:29.920
But there's a real -- there's a real-ass bear in this book.

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:35.980
Was a bear something you wanted to write about for a while?

01:16:36.220 --> 01:16:38.440
Yes, it was. It was, I will say.

01:16:38.840 --> 01:16:41.360
So, one foundational part of this book

01:16:41.360 --> 01:16:43.620
was that there's a fairy tale that I loved as a kid

01:16:43.620 --> 01:16:45.000
called Snow White and Rose Red.

01:16:45.060 --> 01:16:46.180
It's a Brothers Grimm fairy tale.

01:16:46.460 --> 01:16:49.040
And did you read Brothers Grimm fairy tales when you were a kid?

01:16:49.320 --> 01:16:50.480
They're terrifying, right?

01:16:50.520 --> 01:16:50.900
They're terrifying.

01:16:51.450 --> 01:16:52.780
They're very specific.

01:16:53.060 --> 01:16:56.910
They're very weird and dark and violent.

01:16:57.250 --> 01:16:59.550
And I was handed a collection of fairy tales

01:16:59.550 --> 01:17:00.000
when I was,

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:03.960
you know six or seven I thought oh great this isn't gonna scar and shape me

01:17:03.960 --> 01:17:09.740
forever and it did I'm delighted to say and so there was this story called snow

01:17:09.740 --> 01:17:13.080
white and rose red about two sisters who live in a cottage with their mother and

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:16.560
then a bear comes to their door but it's a fairy tale so in that story this

01:17:16.560 --> 01:17:20.770
bear is enchanted and he comes and sleeps on their floor every night and

01:17:20.770 --> 01:17:24.790
they roll around on the ground with him and put their hands in his fur and I

01:17:24.790 --> 01:17:28.070
didn't know as a kid the word perverse but if I had I think I would have said

01:17:28.070 --> 01:17:30.000
"This story is very perverse."

01:17:30.320 --> 01:17:34.020
It was weird and unsettling and disturbing,

01:17:34.020 --> 01:17:35.520
and it stuck with me forever.

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:37.660
And so when I started writing this project,

01:17:37.660 --> 01:17:40.740
it takes those elements that so sort of obsessed

01:17:40.740 --> 01:17:42.340
and disturbed me as a kid

01:17:42.340 --> 01:17:47.380
and tries to fit them into a new structure or a new story.

01:17:47.380 --> 01:17:51.280
That said, a lot of what informed this particular book, too,

01:17:51.280 --> 01:17:54.850
was experiences I've had with bears in my own life.

01:17:54.850 --> 01:17:58.640
-Okay. So a lot of people don't have bear experiences.

01:18:00.410 --> 01:18:02.870
Where would you say you picked up most of your...

01:18:02.870 --> 01:18:04.900
-Bear interactions?

01:18:04.900 --> 01:18:07.200
-I would say most, but not all.

01:18:07.200 --> 01:18:11.060
So you mentioned my first book was set in this region of Russia

01:18:11.060 --> 01:18:13.760
called Kamchatka, which is on the Pacific coast.

01:18:13.760 --> 01:18:14.760
It's kind of like the --

01:18:14.760 --> 01:18:16.680
If you picture the tail of Alaska swinging out,

01:18:16.680 --> 01:18:18.610
it swings into Kamchatka. -Okay.

01:18:18.610 --> 01:18:21.700
-And Kamchatka is home to many, many beautiful

01:18:21.700 --> 01:18:24.300
and exciting and extraordinary things --

01:18:24.300 --> 01:18:28.110
volcanoes and lots of salmon and, you know,

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:30.000
big mountains to go snowboarding on,

01:18:30.030 --> 01:18:31.850
and a lot of bears, a ton of bears,

01:18:31.970 --> 01:18:33.270
a ton of very big brown bears.

01:18:33.870 --> 01:18:36.010
I was there to research my first book,

01:18:36.290 --> 01:18:40.300
and researching a novel is kind of like a pretty sweet gig

01:18:40.300 --> 01:18:42.940
where you just do things,

01:18:42.940 --> 01:18:45.000
and then you say, this is novel research.

01:18:45.140 --> 01:18:46.240
This is what I'm doing.

01:18:46.340 --> 01:18:48.320
Oh, what I'm going to get to be able to tell my kids,

01:18:48.400 --> 01:18:49.500
I'm researching a novel!

01:18:49.640 --> 01:18:51.710
I'm going to have to be snowboarding for a while

01:18:51.710 --> 01:18:52.510
because I'm researching...

01:18:52.510 --> 01:18:53.830
I wasn't snow... I don't know how to snowboard.

01:18:54.290 --> 01:18:57.510
But I volunteered at a nature preserve

01:18:57.510 --> 01:18:58.770
that was very, very isolated.

01:18:59.010 --> 01:19:00.000
It was just...

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:05.550
and two rangers, and many, many bears.

01:19:05.550 --> 01:19:09.350
And we were all being driven mad by this isolation.

01:19:09.350 --> 01:19:11.190
You could only get in and out by helicopter.

01:19:11.190 --> 01:19:12.890
I mean, it was really out there.

01:19:12.890 --> 01:19:14.690
And so the rangers would say, like,

01:19:14.690 --> 01:19:17.340
"You want to go walk up to some brown bears?"

01:19:17.340 --> 01:19:19.140
And we would say, "Yay!"

01:19:19.140 --> 01:19:21.000
And then they would take their rifles

01:19:21.000 --> 01:19:24.440
and escort us, like, 50 feet away, 40 feet away,

01:19:24.440 --> 01:19:30.000
30 feet away from a 9-foot-long bear and her cuss.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:32.940
and we would stare at them, and they have these --

01:19:32.940 --> 01:19:34.660
You know, you can really see from that distance,

01:19:34.660 --> 01:19:37.560
like, the pattern of their fur and the shine of their eyes

01:19:37.560 --> 01:19:41.560
and their teeth and their claws and, like, their yellow bone.

01:19:41.560 --> 01:19:44.160
And I remember thinking, "If this is how I go out,

01:19:44.160 --> 01:19:45.960
it's so glorious, it'll be worth it."

01:19:45.960 --> 01:19:49.830
And then when I got back to my cabin, I thought,

01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:51.720
"I don't -- I don't think that's true.

01:19:51.720 --> 01:19:53.420
I don't think I want that."

01:19:53.420 --> 01:19:55.420
-I just think about the bear cubs being like,

01:19:55.420 --> 01:19:57.630
"Hey, Mom, was that lady crazy?"

01:19:57.630 --> 01:20:00.000
-It really was an intense experience,

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:02.830
And I tried to really put that experience into the book.

01:20:02.830 --> 01:20:03.870
There are these two sisters,

01:20:03.870 --> 01:20:05.370
both of whom have very different reactions

01:20:05.370 --> 01:20:06.330
to the bear that shows up,

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:09.360
and one of them is having a reverent,

01:20:09.360 --> 01:20:11.860
overwhelmingly emotional, ecstatic experience,

01:20:11.860 --> 01:20:13.260
and the other is not.

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:15.760
-It is -- I think that's a good place to leave it,

01:20:15.760 --> 01:20:19.030
because how -- It's a fantastic read.

01:20:19.030 --> 01:20:20.160
That's all I'll say.

01:20:20.160 --> 01:20:21.760
Thank you so much for being here, Julia.

01:20:21.760 --> 01:20:23.320
What a point.

01:20:23.320 --> 01:20:24.280
Julia Phelps, everyone.

01:20:24.280 --> 01:20:26.280
"Bear" is on sale now wherever books are sold.

01:20:26.280 --> 01:20:28.680
But please, support local and independent booksellers.

01:20:28.680 --> 01:20:29.250
We'll be right back.
